     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Tutor to a Future Tyrant",
     "content": "In 49 CE, Seneca returned from exile to tutor the 12-year-old who would become **Nero Claudius Caesar**. He taught rhetoric, philosophy, and statecraft. He wrote Nero's first speech as emperor — a stunning declaration of clemency and senatorial respect.\n\nFor 5 years, Rome flourished. The historian **Trajan** later called these years the best of any emperor. Seneca and **Burrus**, the praetorian prefect, governed in Nero's name.\n\nThen came the unraveling:\n\n**59 CE** — Nero murders his mother **Agrippina**. Seneca writes the cover-up letter to the Senate.\n**62 CE** — Burrus dies. Seneca loses his political shield and asks to retire. Nero refuses.\n**64 CE** — The Great Fire of Rome. Nero blames Christians, builds his Golden House.\n**65 CE** — The **Pisonian Conspiracy** is uncovered. Seneca is implicated, possibly falsely. Nero orders him to die.\n\nThe **Letters** were written in the shadow of all of this. Each one is a man trying to **out-philosophize his own complicity**.",
