     "title": "Recognizing Partial Moral Progress Honestly",
     "content": "Letter 47 is valuable today less as a source of specific ethical instruction, we do not need Seneca to tell us slavery is wrong, and more as a case study in how to evaluate moral progress that is real but incomplete, a pattern that recurs constantly in intellectual and social history. The exercise this lesson offers is a habit of reading historical moral arguments, including celebrated ones, with two questions held simultaneously: what genuine expansion of moral concern is happening here, and what boundary does the argument still take entirely for granted, treating it as too obvious to even mention, let alone defend. Seneca expands moral concern to include the inner life and rational dignity of enslaved people, a real achievement relative to his surrounding culture, while taking the legal category of slavery so entirely for granted that its injustice never surfaces as a question at all.\n\nThis pattern is not confined to antiquity. Many moral advances across history have arrived partially, extending real concern to some, while leaving other boundaries, other categories of person treated as beneath full consideration, invisible to the very reformers making progress elsewhere. The practical habit worth building is to ask, of any moral argument, historical or contemporary, including our own convictions: what does this argument expand, and what does it still silently assume as fixed, unchangeable, or beneath question. This is uncomfortable to apply to yourself, since blind spots are by definition not visible from inside them, but the discipline of asking is itself valuable, and Seneca's letter, precisely because it mixes real moral insight with a total blind spot in one document, is unusually well suited to practicing that discipline before turning it, more searchingly, on your own certainties.",
